      en: "The Iskar is the longest river running entirely within Bulgaria, stretching over 368 kilometers from its source in the Rila Mountains to its mouth at the Danube River. It is formed by the confluence of three smaller rivers and flows northward, carving through the landscape of the Sofia Plain and the Balkan Mountains. Its most visually stunning section is the Iskar Gorge, where the river has created a deep, 70-kilometer-long valley through the mountains, featuring dramatic limestone cliffs and ancient caves. The river is the primary source of drinking water for the capital, Sofia, and feeds the Iskar Reservoir, which is the largest artificial lake in the country by volume. Historically, the Iskar Valley has served as a critical trade and transportation route since Thracian and Roman times, connecting the interior with the Danube basin. Many historic sites, including the Cherepish Monastery, are built directly into the sheer rock walls overlooking the river. In modern times, the Iskar is a popular hub for outdoor activities such as rafting, kayaking, and hiking, with several sections protected for their ecological and scenic value.",
